1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,237
This is ECE 320, video 17 b. We solve the Fall 2022 midterm, all four questions, in order. The file name is f 2020 midterm, but the header says Fall 2022.

2
00:00:17,687 --> 00:00:22,487
Question 1 is Amdahl's law with a slowdown. It is worth 6 marks.

3
00:00:22,937 --> 00:00:31,343
Question 2 is caches. You split the address into tag, index and offset, fill an access table, and improve the cache.

4
00:00:31,793 --> 00:00:37,318
Question 3 is a pipeline diagram with a forwarding table and a stall table.

5
00:00:37,768 --> 00:00:45,597
Question 4 is a pipeline design question. It uses the CLSD pattern and a cache in the memory stage.

6
00:00:46,047 --> 00:00:53,173
Question 1 is about performance. A processor has a multiplication unit, and Alice makes it faster.

7
00:00:53,623 --> 00:01:02,988
In part A, multiplications become 5 times faster. They take 40 percent of the original time. Find the overall speedup.

8
00:01:03,438 --> 00:01:13,806
In part B, the same change makes data cache accesses 3 times slower. They take 30 percent of the original time. Is the program faster?

9
00:01:14,256 --> 00:01:24,773
Draw the original time as one bar of length 1. Multiplication is point 4, cache accesses are point 3, and the other work is point 3.

10
00:01:25,223 --> 00:01:30,514
Pause the video and try it. Find the new time of each part, then add the parts.

11
00:01:30,964 --> 00:01:36,233
Part A. The old time is 1. Multiplication is the marked part, point 4.

12
00:01:36,683 --> 00:01:42,913
The rest is 1 minus point 4, which is point 6. The optimization does not change it.

13
00:01:43,363 --> 00:01:50,637
The multiplication part becomes 5 times faster. Point 4 divided by 5 is point 0 8.

14
00:01:51,087 --> 00:01:57,359
Add the parts. The new time is point 6 plus point 0 8, which is point 6 8.

15
00:01:57,809 --> 00:02:05,404
Speedup is the old time divided by the new time. 1 divided by point 6 8 is 1.47.

16
00:02:05,854 --> 00:02:14,409
This is Amdahl's law. f is the fraction of the old time that becomes faster, here point 4. s is its speedup, 5.

17
00:02:14,859 --> 00:02:19,595
The answer to part A: the overall speedup is about 1.47.

18
00:02:20,045 --> 00:02:28,215
Part B. Draw the old time again: other work point 3, multiplication point 4, and cache accesses point 3.

19
00:02:28,665 --> 00:02:32,185
The other work does not change. It stays point 3.

20
00:02:32,635 --> 00:02:39,846
Multiplication is still 5 times faster. It becomes point 0 8, a gain of point 3 2.

21
00:02:40,296 --> 00:02:48,147
Cache accesses become 3 times slower. Point 3 times 3 is point 9. That is a loss of point 6.

22
00:02:48,597 --> 00:02:55,807
Add the parts. The new time is point 3 plus point 0 8 plus point 9, which is 1.28.

23
00:02:56,257 --> 00:03:03,148
The speedup is 1 divided by 1.28, which is point 7 8. This is less than 1.

24
00:03:03,598 --> 00:03:13,198
This is the general form of Amdahl's law. A slowdown of 3 is a speedup of one third, so that part takes 3 times its old time.

25
00:03:13,648 --> 00:03:26,256
The answer to part B: no. The new time is 1.28 of the old time. The loss of point 6 is larger than the gain of point 3 2. So the program runs slower.

26
00:03:26,706 --> 00:03:41,533
Now the Fall 2022 midterm, question 2. Part A, 3 marks. Two 4-way caches with 16 bit addresses. Find the tag, index and offset bits of both. Pause the video and try it.

27
00:03:41,983 --> 00:03:51,796
Cache 1. 1024 bytes in 16 byte blocks give 1024 divided by 16: 64 blocks.

28
00:03:52,246 --> 00:04:02,507
64 blocks in sets of 4 ways give 64 divided by 4: 16 sets. The grid shows them: 16 rows of 4 ways.

29
00:04:02,957 --> 00:04:08,205
A block has 16 bytes, which is 2 to the 4. So the offset is 4 bits.

30
00:04:08,655 --> 00:04:12,751
16 sets is 2 to the 4, so the index is 4 bits.

31
00:04:13,201 --> 00:04:17,532
The tag is 16 minus 4 minus 4, which is 8 bits.

32
00:04:17,982 --> 00:04:30,227
Cache 2. 16384 bytes in 256 byte blocks also give 64 blocks. So it also has 16 sets of 4 ways.

33
00:04:30,677 --> 00:04:36,757
A block has 256 bytes, which is 2 to the 8. So the offset is 8 bits.

34
00:04:37,207 --> 00:04:40,237
16 sets again give a 4 bit index.

35
00:04:40,687 --> 00:04:44,911
The tag is 16 minus 4 minus 8, which is 4 bits.

36
00:04:45,361 --> 00:04:51,590
Both caches have 16 sets. The larger blocks move 4 bits from the tag to the offset.

37
00:04:52,040 --> 00:05:02,621
Part B, 5 marks. Find which cache gives the higher hit rate for the program in the question. First, find the addresses. Pause the video and try it.

38
00:05:03,071 --> 00:05:13,738
Each row of A has 4096 bytes. That is hex 1 0 0 0. So row j of A starts at hex j 0 0 0.

39
00:05:14,188 --> 00:05:22,401
Each element is 4 bytes. So A of j, i is at 4096 times j plus 4 times i.

40
00:05:22,851 --> 00:05:32,451
B starts at hex 4 0 1 0, after the rows of A. So B of i is at hex 4 0 1 0 plus 4 times i.

41
00:05:32,901 --> 00:05:47,109
The inner loop runs over j. So the program walks down column 0 of A. It writes A of 0, 0, then A of 1, 0, A of 2, 0 and A of 3, 0. Then it writes B of 0.

42
00:05:47,559 --> 00:05:51,677
Then the same for i equal to 1. That gives 10 addresses.

43
00:05:52,127 --> 00:06:02,047
Cache 1 has 16 byte blocks. Its tag is bits 15 to 8, the index is bits 7 to 4, and the offset is bits 3 to 0.

44
00:06:02,497 --> 00:06:17,409
The first four addresses are hex 0, 1 0 0 0, 2 0 0 0 and 3 0 0 0. Their index bits, 7 to 4, are all 0. So all four go to set 0.

45
00:06:17,859 --> 00:06:26,136
Their tags are 0 0, 1 0, 2 0 and 3 0. Four misses fill the 4 ways of set 0.

46
00:06:26,586 --> 00:06:36,186
B of 0 is at hex 4 0 1 0. Its index bits, 7 to 4, are 0 0 0 1. So it goes to set 1.

47
00:06:36,636 --> 00:06:41,009
Its tag is 4 0. Set 1 is empty, so it is a miss.

48
00:06:41,459 --> 00:06:53,150
Now i is 1. A of 0, 1 is at hex 4. Only its offset differs from hex 0. So it is in the same 16 byte block as A of 0, 0. Hit.

49
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:58,549
A of 1, 1, A of 2, 1 and A of 3, 1 hit for the same reason.

50
00:06:58,999 --> 00:07:05,634
B of 1 is at hex 4 0 1 4. Only its offset differs from B of 0. Hit.

51
00:07:06,084 --> 00:07:13,273
Cache 1 has 5 hits in 10 accesses. The hit rate is 5 divided by 10: 50 percent.

52
00:07:13,723 --> 00:07:25,115
Cache 2 has 256 byte blocks. Its tag is bits 15 to 12, the index is bits 11 to 8, and the offset is bits 7 to 0.

53
00:07:25,565 --> 00:07:39,133
Look at hex 0, 1 0 0 0, 2 0 0 0 and 3 0 0 0. Their index bits, 11 to 8, are all 0. So all four go to set 0.

54
00:07:39,583 --> 00:07:45,855
Their tags are 0, 1, 2 and 3. Four misses fill the 4 ways of set 0.

55
00:07:46,305 --> 00:07:57,655
B of 0 is at hex 4 0 1 0. Its index bits, 11 to 8, are also 0 0 0 0. So it also goes to set 0.

56
00:07:58,105 --> 00:08:06,147
Its tag is 4. Set 0 is full. The least recently used block is tag 0, so tag 4 replaces it.

57
00:08:06,597 --> 00:08:12,613
Now A of 0, 1 at hex 4. Its tag is 0, which was just evicted. Miss.

58
00:08:13,063 --> 00:08:18,930
LRU now evicts tag 1. Tag 1 is the next block that the program needs.

59
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:29,556
The same pattern repeats. Five blocks take turns in a 4-way set. LRU always evicts the block that comes next, so every access misses.

60
00:08:30,006 --> 00:08:32,822
Cache 2 has 0 hits in 10 accesses.

61
00:08:33,272 --> 00:08:40,333
So cache 1, with the 16 byte blocks, has the higher hit rate: 50 percent against 0 percent.

62
00:08:40,783 --> 00:08:49,679
Part C, 2 marks. Find a change that gives cache 2 a higher hit rate, with the same cache size. Pause the video and try it.

63
00:08:50,129 --> 00:08:55,655
The problem: 5 blocks map to set 0, but the set has only 4 ways.

64
00:08:56,105 --> 00:09:09,865
Keep 16384 bytes and 256 byte blocks, but make the cache 8-way. 64 blocks in sets of 8 ways give 8 sets. So the index is 3 bits.

65
00:09:10,315 --> 00:09:14,581
The tag is 16 minus 3 minus 8, which is 5 bits.

66
00:09:15,031 --> 00:09:24,482
The 5 blocks still go to set 0, now with the 5 bit tags 0, 2, 4, 6 and 8. All 5 fit in the 8 ways.

67
00:09:24,932 --> 00:09:33,977
So there are 5 misses for i equal to 0, then 5 hits for i equal to 1. 5 hits in 10 is 50 percent.

68
00:09:34,427 --> 00:09:42,662
Smaller blocks also work. With 16 byte blocks, A goes to set 0 and B to set 1: also 50 percent.

69
00:09:43,112 --> 00:09:50,536
So the answer is yes. The misses are conflict misses: raise the associativity, or use smaller blocks.

70
00:09:50,986 --> 00:09:54,975
Question 3 is a pipeline diagram, for 16 marks.

71
00:09:55,425 --> 00:09:58,604
There is forwarding as in class, but none into D.

72
00:09:59,054 --> 00:10:09,977
One rule is new. There is no forwarding into the branch compare unit. So the branch must read both operands in D, after the W of each producer.

73
00:10:10,427 --> 00:10:16,443
Branches resolve in X, and the pipeline guesses not taken until the branch resolves.

74
00:10:16,893 --> 00:10:19,431
The branch in instruction 4 is taken.

75
00:10:19,881 --> 00:10:25,300
A flushed stage gets a dash. Then you fill the forwarding table and the stall table.

76
00:10:25,750 --> 00:10:36,609
The paper writes L W x5, x4 of x1, in number 5 and number 7. That is not valid RISC-V. Read it as an offset of 4.

77
00:10:37,059 --> 00:10:51,160
If you read it as x4 plus x1, the timing does not change, because x4 was written in cycle 5, long before. Write your assumption in the exam. Pause the video and try the question.

78
00:10:51,610 --> 00:11:00,207
Step 1: the producers. Number 1 load word reads x2, and nothing older writes x2. Number 1 writes x4.

79
00:11:00,657 --> 00:11:07,057
Number 2 store word uses x4 as store data. The producer is the load number 1.

80
00:11:07,507 --> 00:11:12,883
Number 3 add reads x4 two times. The producer is also number 1.

81
00:11:13,333 --> 00:11:20,117
Number 4 B E Q compares x4 and x1. x4 comes from the load number 1.

82
00:11:20,567 --> 00:11:22,893
x1 comes from add number 3.

83
00:11:23,343 --> 00:11:24,943
The branch is taken.

84
00:11:25,393 --> 00:11:31,921
So number 5 and number 6 are on the wrong path. They are flushed, and they are not producers.

85
00:11:32,371 --> 00:11:42,419
Number 7 reads x1. Number 6 also writes x1, but number 6 is flushed. So number 7 gets x1 from add number 3.

86
00:11:42,869 --> 00:11:51,487
Number 7 load word writes x5. Number 8 uses x5 as base and as data. The producer is number 7.

87
00:11:51,937 --> 00:11:56,268
Number 9 and number 10 also read x5 from number 7.

88
00:11:56,718 --> 00:12:03,225
Number 11 reads only x0. Number 12 reads x8, and nothing writes x8.

89
00:12:03,675 --> 00:12:12,443
Now the diagram. In cycles 1 to 4, one instruction enters per cycle: the load, the store, the add and the B E Q.

90
00:12:12,893 --> 00:12:28,274
In cycle 5, the load is in W. Number 2 is in M, and it needs x4 as store data. So x4 goes by WM. There is no load-use stall, because store data is needed only in M.

91
00:12:28,724 --> 00:12:36,916
Number 3 is in X, and it needs x4. So x4 goes by WX. One W feeds two forwards.

92
00:12:37,366 --> 00:12:43,787
Number 4 B E Q is in D in cycle 5, and it waits. The next steps show why.

93
00:12:44,237 --> 00:12:52,707
Number 4 B E Q gets no forwarded values. It must read x4 and x1 in D after their W.

94
00:12:53,157 --> 00:12:57,274
x4 is written by the load number 1 in cycle 5.

95
00:12:57,724 --> 00:13:02,353
x1 is written by add number 3 in W, in cycle 7.

96
00:13:02,803 --> 00:13:06,942
So B E Q repeats D in cycles 5, 6 and 7.

97
00:13:07,392 --> 00:13:10,443
Number 5 repeats F in the same cycles.

98
00:13:10,893 --> 00:13:16,375
In cycle 8, B E Q reads x4 and x1 from the register file.

99
00:13:16,825 --> 00:13:20,111
Number 5 does its last F in cycle 8.

100
00:13:20,561 --> 00:13:25,019
In cycle 9, B E Q is in X. It resolves: taken.

101
00:13:25,469 --> 00:13:33,043
The pipeline guessed not taken. So number 5, now in D, and number 6, now in F, are on the wrong path.

102
00:13:33,493 --> 00:13:38,335
In cycle 10, number 5 and number 6 are flushed. They get dashes.

103
00:13:38,785 --> 00:13:42,540
T1, instruction 7, is fetched in cycle 10.

104
00:13:42,990 --> 00:13:59,203
Number 7 needs x1. Number 6 also writes x1, but number 6 is flushed. So the producer is add number 3. It wrote x1 in cycle 7. Number 7 reads x1 in D in cycle 11. No stall.

105
00:13:59,653 --> 00:14:13,840
Number 8 store word uses x5 as base and as data. The producer is the load number 7. In cycle 12, number 8 is in D. Its X would be cycle 13, while the load is in M.

106
00:14:14,290 --> 00:14:20,306
The base is needed in X. This is load-use, so number 8 stalls in cycle 12.

107
00:14:20,756 --> 00:14:30,335
In cycle 13, number 8 tries again. Its X would be cycle 14, while the load is in W. The base could take WX.

108
00:14:30,785 --> 00:14:38,678
But the store data has no WX path in the class datapath. So number 8 stalls again in cycle 13.

109
00:14:39,128 --> 00:14:52,653
In cycle 14, the load writes x5 in W. Number 8 is in D in the same cycle, so it reads the old x5. This is the W D case, and D repeats once more.

110
00:14:53,103 --> 00:14:57,519
Number 9 waits in F in cycles 12, 13 and 14.

111
00:14:57,969 --> 00:15:02,748
In cycle 15, number 8 reads x5 from the register file.

112
00:15:03,198 --> 00:15:14,398
Count the D star cells of number 8: 3 stall cycles. Each has a different reason: load-use, no WX path for store data, and the W D case.

113
00:15:14,848 --> 00:15:26,389
Number 9 store word needs x5 as store data. The load wrote x5 in cycle 14. Number 9 reads it in D in cycle 16. No stall.

114
00:15:26,839 --> 00:15:32,706
Number 10 reads x5 from the register file in cycle 17. No stall.

115
00:15:33,156 --> 00:15:49,860
Number 11 and number 12 do not read x5. They only write it. The writes of number 10, number 11 and number 12 come in program order, in cycles 20, 21 and 22. So these write after write pairs are safe.

116
00:15:50,310 --> 00:15:52,678
The run ends in cycle 22.

117
00:15:53,128 --> 00:16:05,352
Now the two tables. There are only two forwards, both from the load number 1 in cycle 5. Row 1: x4 goes from W to M, into the store data of number 2.

118
00:16:05,802 --> 00:16:10,069
Row 2: x4 goes from W to X, into number 3.

119
00:16:10,519 --> 00:16:18,775
The stall table. Number 4 stalls in D in cycles 5, 6 and 7, because the compare unit gets no forwarding.

120
00:16:19,225 --> 00:16:29,081
Number 5 stalls in F in the same cycles. Number 5 is flushed later, but its F stars are real stalls. Write them in the table.

121
00:16:29,531 --> 00:16:42,181
Number 8 stalls in D in cycles 12, 13 and 14. Write the three reasons: load-use for the base, no WX path for the store data, and the W D case.

122
00:16:42,631 --> 00:16:45,789
Number 9 stalls in F in the same cycles.

123
00:16:46,239 --> 00:16:57,524
Here is the whole diagram. The red rings mark the 6 D star cycles: number 4 in cycles 5 to 7, and number 8 in cycles 12 to 14.

124
00:16:57,974 --> 00:17:17,387
The answer to question 3. The run takes 22 cycles. There are two forwards, both from the load number 1 in cycle 5. Number 4 and number 8 stall in D for 3 cycles each, and the instruction behind each one stalls in F. Number 5 and number 6 are flushed.

125
00:17:17,837 --> 00:17:24,771
Now question 4 of the fall 2022 paper. It uses the class pipeline with forwarding.

126
00:17:25,221 --> 00:17:27,205
There is no forwarding into D.

127
00:17:27,655 --> 00:17:31,196
There is also no forwarding into the branch compare unit.

128
00:17:31,646 --> 00:17:39,219
A C L S D pattern is a sequence of consecutive loads that write the same destination register.

129
00:17:39,669 --> 00:17:46,517
In program P, numbers 1 and 2 both load into x 1. This is a write after write pair.

130
00:17:46,967 --> 00:17:50,125
Nobody reads the x 1 value of number 1.

131
00:17:50,575 --> 00:17:56,825
Number 4 reads x 1. Its producer is number 2, the latest writer of x 1.

132
00:17:57,275 --> 00:18:03,547
Part A asks you to execute program P and explain every stall. Pause the video and try it.

133
00:18:03,997 --> 00:18:14,088
The instructions enter the pipeline one per cycle. Number 1 enters in cycle 1, number 2 in cycle 2, number 3 in cycle 3.

134
00:18:14,538 --> 00:18:19,935
Number 1 computes its address, x 4 plus 0, in X in cycle 3.

135
00:18:20,385 --> 00:18:27,489
Number 1 reads D mem in M, in cycle 4. Its value exists at the end of cycle 4.

136
00:18:27,939 --> 00:18:36,195
Number 3 reads x 3 in D, in cycle 4. No instruction before it writes x 3, so there is no hazard.

137
00:18:36,645 --> 00:18:39,419
Number 4 is in X in cycle 6.

138
00:18:39,869 --> 00:18:48,637
Number 2, the latest writer of x 1, is in W in cycle 6. So x 1 goes by WX. There is no stall.

139
00:18:49,087 --> 00:18:55,551
Program P takes 8 cycles with no stalls. So the stall explanation is: there are none.

140
00:18:56,001 --> 00:19:00,715
The work of number 1 is wasted, because number 2 overwrites x 1.

141
00:19:01,165 --> 00:19:07,032
Part B adds a cache in M. The data memory is a 2-way set associative cache.

142
00:19:07,482 --> 00:19:09,146
It has 16 sets.

143
00:19:09,596 --> 00:19:11,687
Each block has 16 bytes.

144
00:19:12,137 --> 00:19:18,963
It uses L R U replacement. A larger age means that the block was used less recently.

145
00:19:19,413 --> 00:19:22,933
M stalls on a miss. A hit takes 1 cycle.

146
00:19:23,383 --> 00:19:26,327
A miss with a vacant way takes 2 cycles.

147
00:19:26,777 --> 00:19:30,937
A miss in a full set evicts a block and takes 4 cycles.

148
00:19:31,387 --> 00:19:36,635
The table shows the cache at the start: sets 0 to 3, each with two ways.

149
00:19:37,085 --> 00:19:41,565
At the start of program P, x 4 holds hex 410.

150
00:19:42,015 --> 00:19:44,831
And x 5 holds hex 610.

151
00:19:45,281 --> 00:19:55,201
Part B asks for the pipeline diagram, the final registers and cache, and the M latency of numbers 1 and 2. Pause the video and try it.

152
00:19:55,651 --> 00:20:03,395
First, split each address. A block has 16 bytes, and 16 is 2 to the 4. So the offset has 4 bits.

153
00:20:03,845 --> 00:20:09,648
The cache has 16 sets, and 16 is 2 to the 4. So the index has 4 bits.

154
00:20:10,098 --> 00:20:15,837
The tag is the rest: 32 minus 4 minus 4, which is 24 bits.

155
00:20:16,287 --> 00:20:28,703
Number 1 loads from hex 410. Each hex digit gives 4 bits: 0 1 0 0, then 0 0 0 1, then 0 0 0 0.

156
00:20:29,153 --> 00:20:33,142
The 4 offset bits are all 0, so the offset is 0.

157
00:20:33,592 --> 00:20:38,947
The 4 index bits are 0 0 0 1, so number 1 uses set 1.

158
00:20:39,397 --> 00:20:45,797
The tag bits are 0 1 0 0, with zeros above them. So the tag is hex 4.

159
00:20:46,247 --> 00:20:58,961
Number 2 loads from hex 610. Its bits are 0 1 1 0, then 0 0 0 1, then 0 0 0 0. Its offset is also 0.

160
00:20:59,411 --> 00:21:05,662
Its index bits are also 0 0 0 1, so number 2 also uses set 1.

161
00:21:06,112 --> 00:21:10,507
Its tag bits are 0 1 1 0, so the tag is hex 6.

162
00:21:10,957 --> 00:21:12,855
So both loads use set 1.

163
00:21:13,305 --> 00:21:17,039
Set 1 of the given cache holds tag hex 6 in way 0.

164
00:21:17,489 --> 00:21:19,643
Way 1 holds tag hex 8.

165
00:21:20,093 --> 00:21:26,493
Number 2 has tag hex 6. So the block of number 2 is already in the cache, in way 0.

166
00:21:26,943 --> 00:21:36,373
Now run the two loads through set 1. At the start, way 0 has age 1. So way 0 is the least recently used block.

167
00:21:36,823 --> 00:21:46,551
Number 1 looks for tag hex 4. Way 0 holds tag hex 6, and way 1 holds tag hex 8. No way matches, so it is a miss.

168
00:21:47,001 --> 00:21:55,278
Both ways are valid, so the set is full. L R U picks way 0, the larger age, and evicts tag hex 6.

169
00:21:55,728 --> 00:21:58,501
A miss in a full set takes 4 cycles.

170
00:21:58,951 --> 00:22:03,623
Then the ages change. The used way, way 0, gets age 0.

171
00:22:04,073 --> 00:22:12,457
Way 1 had age 0, lower than the old age of way 0, which was 1. So way 1 gets plus 1: age 1.

172
00:22:12,907 --> 00:22:20,395
Number 2 looks for tag hex 6. The set now holds tags hex 4 and hex 8. So this is a miss too.

173
00:22:20,845 --> 00:22:27,245
Way 1 now has the larger age, 1. So L R U evicts way 1, tag hex 8.

174
00:22:27,695 --> 00:22:33,477
The ages change again. Way 1 gets age 0, and way 0 gets age 1.

175
00:22:33,927 --> 00:22:36,487
The latency is again 4 cycles.

176
00:22:36,937 --> 00:22:43,187
This is the important point. The dead load, number 1, evicted the block that number 2 needs.

177
00:22:43,637 --> 00:22:50,144
Number 1 enters M in cycle 4. It misses in a full set, so M takes 4 cycles.

178
00:22:50,594 --> 00:22:55,735
M is busy, so number 2 cannot enter M. It repeats X: X star.

179
00:22:56,185 --> 00:23:04,036
Number 1 stays in M in cycles 4, 5, 6 and 7. Every repeat except the last gets a star.

180
00:23:04,486 --> 00:23:08,134
Number 3 repeats D in cycles 4, 5 and 6.

181
00:23:08,584 --> 00:23:11,592
Number 4 repeats F in the same cycles.

182
00:23:12,042 --> 00:23:15,605
Number 1 writes x 1 in W, in cycle 8.

183
00:23:16,055 --> 00:23:24,012
Number 2 enters M in cycle 8. It also misses in a full set, so it stays in M for cycles 8 to 11.

184
00:23:24,462 --> 00:23:28,302
Number 2 writes x 1 in W in cycle 12.

185
00:23:28,752 --> 00:23:36,069
Number 4 is in X in cycle 12. So x 1 goes by WX, with no extra stall.

186
00:23:36,519 --> 00:23:39,079
Program P now takes 14 cycles.

187
00:23:39,529 --> 00:23:45,268
Part A took 8 cycles. So the misses add 14 minus 8, which is 6 cycles.

188
00:23:45,718 --> 00:23:51,265
The M latency is 4 cycles for number 1, and 4 cycles for number 2.

189
00:23:51,715 --> 00:23:59,544
The final registers. x 1 holds the word at address hex 610, the value that number 2 loads.

190
00:23:59,994 --> 00:24:06,586
x 3 was hex 2 F 0. Number 3 adds 1, so x 3 is hex 2 F 1.

191
00:24:07,036 --> 00:24:12,519
Number 4 adds 1 to x 1. So x 6 is that memory word plus 1.

192
00:24:12,969 --> 00:24:15,123
The other registers do not change.

193
00:24:15,573 --> 00:24:18,752
The final cache. Only set 1 changes.

194
00:24:19,202 --> 00:24:23,234
In set 1, way 0 holds tag hex 4 with age 1.

195
00:24:23,684 --> 00:24:26,649
Way 1 holds tag hex 6 with age 0.

196
00:24:27,099 --> 00:24:31,878
The M latency of number 1 is 4 cycles: a miss in a full set.

197
00:24:32,328 --> 00:24:38,493
Number 2 also takes 4 cycles. It misses, because number 1 evicted its block.

198
00:24:38,943 --> 00:24:48,522
The exam gives no memory values. So write x 1 as the memory word at hex 610, and x 6 as that word plus 1.

199
00:24:48,972 --> 00:25:00,833
Part C asks for the side effects, and if each one helps or hurts. Pause the video and try it. If every access hits, program P takes 8 cycles, as in Part A.

200
00:25:01,283 --> 00:25:07,918
Side effect one. Number 1 misses: 4 cycles in M, 3 more than a 1-cycle hit.

201
00:25:08,368 --> 00:25:16,027
Nobody reads the x 1 value of number 1. So M is busy for a dead value. This degrades performance.

202
00:25:16,477 --> 00:25:24,157
Side effect two. Number 1 changes the cache. It evicts block hex 6, the block that number 2 needs.

203
00:25:24,607 --> 00:25:30,537
So number 2 misses too, and adds 3 more cycles. This also degrades performance.

204
00:25:30,987 --> 00:25:36,555
In total, 8 plus 3 plus 3 is 14 cycles, the result of Part B.

205
00:25:37,005 --> 00:25:46,776
Side effect three. In another program, the block of number 1 can be useful. A later load to that block then hits. This benefits performance.

206
00:25:47,226 --> 00:25:53,178
Without the access of number 1, number 2 hits, because block hex 6 stays in the cache.

207
00:25:53,628 --> 00:25:56,849
Program P then takes 8 cycles, not 14.

208
00:25:57,299 --> 00:26:07,475
Part D asks for a change to the pipeline control that improves the C L S D pattern. Pause the video and try it. Then look at cycle 3.

209
00:26:07,925 --> 00:26:12,683
In cycle 3, number 2 is in D. The F D register holds it.

210
00:26:13,133 --> 00:26:17,037
Number 1 is in X. The D X register holds it.

211
00:26:17,487 --> 00:26:23,183
The class pipeline already has a kill or no op mux before D X, for stalls.

212
00:26:23,633 --> 00:26:28,859
Add a new check to the hazard unit. First, the instruction in X is a load.

213
00:26:29,309 --> 00:26:31,976
Second, the instruction in D is a load.

214
00:26:32,426 --> 00:26:34,495
Third, both have the same rd.

215
00:26:34,945 --> 00:26:38,081
Fourth, the load in D does not read that rd.

216
00:26:38,531 --> 00:26:45,977
Add a new mux before the X M register. Its second input is a no op: an instruction that does nothing.

217
00:26:46,427 --> 00:26:53,317
If all four checks are true, the hazard unit sets a kill signal. Then the new mux selects the no op.

218
00:26:53,767 --> 00:26:58,823
In cycle 4, the X M register holds the no op, not number 1.

219
00:26:59,273 --> 00:27:07,871
The no op does not access the cache, and it writes no register in W. So number 1 shows dashes in M and W.

220
00:27:08,321 --> 00:27:19,115
Do not kill number 1 if number 2 reads rd. For example, load word x 1, 0 of x 1 needs the x 1 value of number 1.

221
00:27:19,565 --> 00:27:29,123
One more caution. If number 1 had a bad address, it would raise an exception. The kill removes that exception. Mention this in the exam.

222
00:27:29,573 --> 00:27:38,639
Now run program P with the kill. In cycle 3, number 1 is in X and number 2 is in D. Both are loads to x 1.

223
00:27:39,089 --> 00:27:44,039
Number 2 does not read x 1. So the hazard unit kills number 1.

224
00:27:44,489 --> 00:27:49,481
Number 1 shows dashes in M and W. It does not access the cache.

225
00:27:49,931 --> 00:27:53,835
Number 2 accesses set 1 in M, in cycle 5.

226
00:27:54,285 --> 00:27:58,509
Its tag, hex 6, is still in way 0. So this is a hit.

227
00:27:58,959 --> 00:28:01,007
A hit takes 1 cycle in M.

228
00:28:01,457 --> 00:28:05,723
Then way 0 gets age 0, and way 1 gets age 1.

229
00:28:06,173 --> 00:28:12,573
Number 4 gets x 1 from number 2 by WX, in cycle 6, as in Part A.

230
00:28:13,023 --> 00:28:21,855
Program P takes 8 cycles. Part B took 14 cycles. So the kill saves 14 minus 8, which is 6 cycles.

231
00:28:22,305 --> 00:28:31,244
The registers end with the same values. So yes, changes to the pipeline control can improve the C L S D pattern.

232
00:28:31,694 --> 00:28:42,190
The answers to question 4. Part A: program P takes 8 cycles with no stall. Number 4 gets x 1 from number 2 by W X.

233
00:28:42,640 --> 00:28:56,443
Part B: both loads miss in a full set, so each spends 4 cycles in M. The run takes 14 cycles. Set 1 ends with tag hex 4 in way 0 and tag hex 6 in way 1.

234
00:28:56,893 --> 00:29:06,343
Part C: the dead load keeps M busy and evicts the block of the next load. Both degrade performance. Its block can help a later load.

235
00:29:06,793 --> 00:29:18,164
Part D: yes. The hazard unit kills the first load when both loads write the same rd and the second does not read it. Program P then takes 8 cycles.

236
00:29:18,614 --> 00:29:26,038
Recap. Question 1: draw the old time as a bar. Divide each part by its speedup, and add the parts.

237
00:29:26,488 --> 00:29:35,512
Question 2: sets are blocks divided by ways. Then find the offset, index and tag bits, and trace each set with LRU.

238
00:29:35,962 --> 00:29:46,799
Question 3: find the producers first. With no forwarding into D, the consumer waits for the producer's W. A taken branch flushes 2 instructions.

239
00:29:47,249 --> 00:30:01,287
Question 4: run program P, then add the cache in M. A miss stalls M. The first load is dead, so the pipeline can kill it. That is the end of video 17 b. Good luck in the midterm.
